        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {warm.measurements.map((measurement) => (
            <tr>
              <td>
                <span
                  class:list={['vendor-dot', vendorColorClass(measurement.tool)]}
                  aria-hidden="true"></span>
                {vendorName(measurement.tool)}
              </td>
              <td class="muted">
                {measurement.supersededA15 ? (
                  <s>{formatMs(measurement.supersededA15.leastSquaresMs)}</s>
                ) : (
                  <em>withheld</em>
                )}
              </td>
              <td>
                <strong>{formatRange(measurement.leastSquaresRangeMs)}</strong>
              </td>
              <td>
                {measurement.supersededA15 ? (
                  <code>{measurement.supersededA15.path}</code>
                ) : (
                  <code>{`${'reports/raw/warm-latency'}/semgrep-java-stability-probe/`}</code>
                )}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="caption">
      <strong>Why the rule was replaced.</strong> "Agree closely" named no
      tolerance, and the only moment at which one could have been chosen was
      after the spreads were known — the after-the-fact decision this tier's
      contract refuses. It was found by measuring again: two repeats on a quiet
      machine disagreed by 12% where two under load had agreed to 5%, and no
      tolerance could have been written down at that point without being fitted
      to spreads already seen. Publishing the interval that was measured needs
      no tolerance at all.
    </p>

    <p class="caption">
      <strong>Why Joern's number moved, and why Semgrep's exists at all.</strong>{' '}
      Machine conditions. Both runs recorded the one-minute load average before
      every batch, so the comparison is read off the artifacts rather than
      recalled:{' '}
      {warm.measurements
        .filter((measurement) => measurement.supersededA15)
        .map((measurement) => (
          <>
            {vendorName(measurement.tool)} at{' '}
            <strong>{loadRange(measurement.supersededA15!.loads)}</strong> when
            A15 measured it, against{' '}
            <strong>{loadSummary(measurement)}</strong> for the figure above.{' '}
          </>
        ))}
      That is most of the difference in Joern's row. It is the whole of
      Semgrep's: A15 withheld a Semgrep figure because its two runs' slopes
      differed by a factor of two, and recorded that as a property of the
      measurement's subject. Re-measured on an idle machine, Semgrep's two
      repeats agree to about 5% — tighter than Joern's.{' '}
      <strong>The instability was the machine, not the engine</strong>, and
      attributing it to Semgrep was a mistake, recorded here rather than quietly
      dropped. It is also why the observed load now travels with every figure on
      this page.
    </p>

    <p class="caption">
      Worth stating plainly, since this benchmark is published by the vendor of
      one of the engines on it: both corrections move numbers{' '}
      <strong>against</strong> that vendor. Joern's marginal falls by about a
      third, and a competitor's withheld figure is restored — and turns out to
      be the fastest warm marginal here. The rule was replaced because it was
      unsound, not because of which way it pointed.
    </p>

    {warm.measurements.map((measurement) => (
      <details class="data-table">
        <summary>
          {`Show the batch series — ${vendorName(measurement.tool)} on the ${
            measurement.language
          } kernel, all ${measurement.runs.length} retained repeats`}
        </summary>
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Cases in the batch (<em>k</em>)</th>
                {measurement.runs.map((run) => (
                  <th>Run {run.run} — wall-clock of the one process</th>
                ))}
                <th>Average per case at run {measurement.runs.length} (not the reported figure)</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {measurement.runs[0]!.batches.map((batch, index) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{batch.k}</td>
                  {measurement.runs.map((run) => (
                    <td><strong>{formatMs(run.batches[index]!.wallMs)}</strong></td>
                  ))}
                  <td class="muted">
                    {formatMs(
                      measurement.runs[measurement.runs.length - 1]!.batches[
                        index
                      ]!.wallMs / batch.k,
                    )}
                  </td>
                </tr>
              ))}
              <tr>
                <td><strong>slope (least squares)</strong></td>
                {measurement.runs.map((run) => (
                  <td><strong>{formatMs(run.leastSquaresMs)}</strong></td>
                ))}
                <td class="muted">—</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="caption">
          The average-per-case column is shown precisely so it can be
          discarded: it falls with <em>k</em> because it is still paying a
          shrinking share of the fixed cost, and it would keep falling with a
          larger <em>k</em> without ever being the marginal cost. The reported
          figure is the slope of a wall-clock column against the first, and the
          published figure is the range those slopes span.
          {measurement.restriction && (
            <>
              {' '}
              <strong>Population:</strong> {measurement.restriction}.
            </>
          )}
          {measurement.environment && (
            <>
              {' '}
              Measured on <code>{measurement.environment.hardwareModel}</code>,{' '}
              <code>{measurement.environment.os} {measurement.environment.osRelease}</code>,{' '}
              {measurement.environment.cpuCount} logical CPUs — the same stamp
              rule as every other number here, and not comparable across
              machines.
            </>
          )}
          {loadSummary(measurement) && (
            <>
              {' '}
              <strong>Machine conditions:</strong> the one-minute load average
              sampled immediately before each batch ranged{' '}
              {loadSummary(measurement)} across every batch of every repeat, on
              a {measurement.environment?.cpuCount ?? '?'}-core machine. The
              tier's hygiene rule is that no other analyzer competes for the
              machine while one is timed, and background load is reported
              rather than asserted away — so the reader can weigh the
              conditions instead of taking "quiet machine" on trust.
            </>
          )}
        </p>
      </details>
    ))}
  </>
)}

